<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <!--
            THE CRON SERVICE USER (#347)

            ir.cron rows without an explicit user_id run as SUPERUSER. Odoo sets
            su=True for uid 1, and su short-circuits the ENTIRE access-check
            machinery — check_access(), has_access() and _filtered_access() all
            return before our LicenseEnforcementMixin._check_access override is
            ever called, and search() bypasses ACLs outright. So Ring 2 could
            not deny any installed model to any cron, whatever the plan said.

            That is why this is a USER and not a flag. The hole cannot be closed
            by patching the licence hook: the bypass lives above it, in core, by
            design. The only place the check can fire is a cron that is not
            superuser in the first place.

            DELIBERATELY NOT base.group_system. LicenseEnforcementMixin exempts
            `self.env.user._is_system()` as well as su, so granting system here
            would reintroduce the exact bug through the other half of the same
            condition. A plain internal user is the point.

            Rights therefore MIRROR THE TENANT'S ENTITLEMENT: this user reaches
            what the tenant's plan allows, automatically, because it goes
            through the ordinary licence path that already works for real users.
            When a tenant downgrades, the cron loses the same models its users
            lose. No second copy of the licensing rules — Standing Rule
            "licensing is inherited, never re-implemented".

            noupdate="1" so a tenant that adjusts this user (extra role groups
            for a cron that legitimately needs them) keeps the change across
            upgrades.
        -->
        <record id="user_cron_service" model="res.users">
            <field name="name">NCollection Scheduler</field>
            <field name="login">cron@ncollection.internal</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <!-- Odoo 19: group_ids, not groups_id. base.group_user ONLY —
                 see the comment above about group_system. -->
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('base.group_user')])]"/>
            <!-- No password and no API key: this account is never logged into
                 and never authenticates. It exists to give scheduled work an
                 identity that the licence layer can actually see. -->
            <field name="password">False</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            WHICH CRONS BIND TO THIS, AND WHY NOT ALL OF THEM.

            Bound: the two anomaly crons in this module. They read ACROSS
            licensable models to detect anomalies, which is exactly the traffic
            Ring 2 is supposed to gate, and is how #347 was found (#61).

            NOT bound: ncollection_auth's log-retention purge. It touches only
            that module's own models in the `ncollection` namespace, which is
            never blocked, so binding it would gain no enforcement while adding
            ACL burden — and ncollection_auth does not depend on this module, so
            it could not resolve this xmlid without introducing a new
            inter-module dependency for no benefit.

            NOT bound: everything in ncollection_saas (backup, config-sync
            reconcile, fleet migration, domain SSL, ECB rates). Those run in the
            PLATFORM database, where this module is not installed at all, so the
            licence mixin does not exist there. Verified: saastest carries
            ncollection_saas + _subscription and NOT ncollection_core; a tenant
            carries ncollection_core and neither platform module. They are
            unaffected by construction, not by care.

            The rule for anything added later: bind a cron to this user if it
            reads models the tenant's PLAN governs. A cron that only touches
            core `ncollection.*` models does not need it.
        -->

        <!-- An immutable anchor. `login` can be renamed by anyone with
             res.users write access in the tenant; an xmlid cannot be moved by
             editing a field, so the crons below resolve the account by xmlid
             and a rename cannot silently detach them (the same reasoning
             config_sync_key.py applies to its own service account). -->
    </data>
</odoo>
